# 170 · One hierarchy: each thing identified and addressed through what owns it, so a run's history stays whole wherever it runs

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.1](../roadmap.md#v011) · **Depends on:** [160](_completed/160-v010-audit.goal)

## What

Thermite's things form one hierarchy: machines host rigs; campaigns, with their kits, have runs; a run has lanes, trials, experiments, attempts, verdicts, reviews, hypotheses, claims, notes and reports. Today some of them are identified or addressed through the wrong parent. An attempt's page, for example, is at `/ui/rigs/<rig>/attempts/<attempt>`: it hangs from the rig, not from its run.

1. **A review of the hierarchy,** designed and as built: each thing's ID, who mints it, how far it is unique, where it is stored, and how every interface addresses it (dashboard URLs, REST routes, MCP tools, the command line, events, signed commands, knowledge-base references, exports and git refs).
2. **One rule:** each thing has an ID that is unique where it is used, and is addressed through what owns it. The rig a trial or an attempt ran on is a fact about it, not its parent.
3. **The changes,** so that a run's history stays whole if the run moves to another rig, or a new run starts from another rig's champion (goal [085](085-move-a-campaign-to-another-rig.goal)): the IDs, the URLs and routes, the stored keys, and the names of the message bus's subjects (goal [168](168-message-bus.goal)).

## Why

The owner, 2026-10-26, about an attempt's page on control: "This is the url seems to say an attempt is tied to the rig, not the run itself... So if the run shifts to a new rig (pause, migrate, start) in the futuer the attempt hierarchy would be broken. Lets double check in our audit the hierarchy of elements and data elements like this and machine>rig>campaigns|kits>runs>experiments|reviews|attempts|etc..."

## Constraints

- **Nothing is destroyed.** The ledger stays the record; a move never rewrites a run's past.
- **v0.1.1 may break compatibility with v0.1.0** (owner, 2026-10-26): IDs, URLs and routes may change; old ledgers are kept, never deleted.
- **Docs follow the code,** in the same commit: the glossary, the REST and MCP references and the pages that show URLs.

## Done when

- [ ] The review is in `.internal/reviews/v0.1.1-audit/` (lane 13), and the rule and its design are recorded here.
- [ ] Every thing is identified and addressed by the rule, in the dashboard, REST, MCP, the command line, events, exports and the knowledge base; a test fails when a route addresses a thing through a parent that does not own it.
- [ ] A run's pages, links and records stay whole when the run continues on another rig, shown by a test.
- [ ] The fast checks, the docs checks and the control, steering and RunPod acceptance checks pass.

## What the review found

The audit's lane 13 (`.internal/reviews/v0.1.1-audit/13-hierarchy.md`) reviewed the hierarchy on 2026-10-26.

- **A run's history is already lost on control after a full reset or a retire,** with no move at all. Control builds every run, attempt and Live view from the rig's current ledger, and lists only rigs that are not retired; every RunPod terminate retires its rig. So the old runs' pages, attempt pages, champion downloads, `get_run` and "copy the queue from this run" stop working, while control still holds every event. An old Live link shows another run.
- **Control has no run of its own.** What it shows is built as Rig ▸ Run ▸ Attempt; the designed Campaign ▸ Run ▸ Lane ▸ Trial ▸ Attempt is only in the breadcrumbs.
- **Run names are unique per rig only,** and two rigs with the same run name is the documented normal case. Usage asked for by run name alone adds up two different runs, and a briefing item is dropped when two rigs used the name.
- **A run name can come back on the same rig after a full reset** (the command line takes any name), and then the results repository and control keep the old run's champion patch: the owner's download serves the wrong champion.
- **A run has nine address shapes, and an attempt eight,** across the dashboard, REST, MCP, the command line, commands, the knowledge base and git. Control has no REST or MCP read of one attempt.
- **Ten event kinds about an attempt or a judge's job don't name their run,** `verdict` among them, so a run's export leaves out its verdicts.
- **Attempt, trial and experiment IDs are already random and unique in practice.** The problem is how things are addressed, not how they are minted.

## Design (proposed by the review)

- **Two trees that meet at the run.** What is done: control ▸ campaign ▸ run ▸ lane ▸ trial ▸ attempt ▸ verdict. Where it is done: control ▸ place (machine, cluster slot, cloud pod) ▸ rig ▸ devices. A run's rig, and the rig each attempt, trial and job ran on, are attributes.
- **Run IDs minted once, by control,** unique across all rigs and ledgers: the readable `<campaign>-<n>`, refused when already used, on every path.
- **One address for each thing:** `/ui/runs/<run>`, `/ui/runs/<run>/attempts/<attempt>`, `/v1/runs/<run>/…`, MCP `{run}` (with a new `get_attempt`), and the same in commands and the knowledge base. Rig-level things (checks, settings, the judge's queue, builds, exports, resets) keep the rig's address.
- **A run registry on control,** written when control signs an assignment, and **views built by run,** across old ledgers and retired rigs.
- **Every run-scoped event names its run.**
- **A move is a new run that continues the old one** (goal [085](085-move-a-campaign-to-another-rig.goal)), shown on both pages.
- **The bus's subjects follow it** (goal [168](168-message-bus.goal)): `thermite.run.<run>.attempt.<attempt>.<kind>`, with the rig and the ledger in headers.
- **The steps,** in goal 125's order: the rule of shape; run IDs unique per control and the right champion patch; a run's export with its verdicts; `core/ids.ts`; the run on every event; the registry; views by run; run-scoped addresses; the knowledge base keyed by run; the subjects; the move (goal 085).

## Decisions

## Open questions

Each with the review's recommendation.

1. **When a run's work moves to another rig, is it the same run, or a new run that continues it?** Recommended: a new run that continues it, with "continues X" and "continued as Y" on both pages. A run's baseline, noise and gains are measured on one rig's devices, and it keeps the decision that a run is one campaign on one rig.
2. **How are run IDs made unique?** Recommended: keep the readable `<campaign>-<n>`, numbered across all rigs and ledgers on control, and refuse a used name on every path.
3. **Dots in campaign and run names.** Recommended: v0.1.1 allows `[a-z0-9_-]` in campaign and run names, with no dot, since a dot splits a bus subject; repositories and kits keep theirs.
4. **The dashboard's address for a run.** Recommended: `/ui/runs/<run>`, `/ui/runs/<run>/<tab>`, `/ui/runs/<run>/attempts/<attempt>`, with the breadcrumbs Campaigns › campaign › run › attempt.
5. **Runs of a retired rig, or from before a full reset.** Recommended: under the campaign's Earlier runs, each marked "on <rig>, retired" or "before <rig>'s full reset", and openable like any run.
6. **Old links after the change.** Recommended: no redirects in v0.1.1, which breaks compatibility by decision.

